{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792424906,"nonce":"pnaK7NHn/MuJpdAFcJcFTK0h19k=","message":"Landed in b3141ab, with fixtures DamagePixels and DamageScrollPixels (partial and whole redraws match pixel for pixel at 1x and 2x).\n\n**Where it applies**\n\nOnly a frame that has a backdrop filter, because only that frame is drawn into a layer that stays. The dashboard has none on any page, so every frame there is drawn whole; the benchmark over its eight pages (offscreen, 1600x820 at 2x) showed no frame drawn in part and no change in time either way.\n\n**Scroll contents, icons, text**\n\n- An icon's tint or a bar in a row: a rect of the icon's size (32x32 at 1x) or the bar's, widened where a backdrop's blur reaches it.\n- A list scrolled: every row's record moves, so the damage is the list's box, joined with the glass over it. In the fixture's 480x300 scene that is often more than 60% and the frame is drawn whole; scrolled a pixel it is drawn in parts.\n- A label that gets longer: the glyph records are more in number, so the records no longer line up and the frame is drawn whole. Typing in an editor does this on every key.\n\n**Next**\n\n- Keep a layer for frames with no backdrop when the last frames changed little, so an app like RayDE benefits.\n- Line up records by content when their count changes, so inserting a glyph or a row damages only what it touches.","files":null}]}